hey guys how's it going so in this video  we're going to be talking about a book  that i read last week called deep work  by cal newport where he basically argues  that the ability to do  deep work is the most valuable  and the most rare skill and the super  competitive economy where businesses  would rather hire machines than people  and where businesses can basically hire  anyone around the world to do the work  [Music]  hi guys welcome back to the channel  if you're new here my name is dami i'm  an architect based in vancouver bc  on this channel i talk about  architecture design and productivity  and if you're interested in any of these  topics please consider subscribing  so in the book deep work count newport  goes into describing why deep work is so  important in this very distracted  society  and he also gives us five actionable  steps to  foster this ability to do deep work so  deep work is the ability to focus on  specific tasks  without distraction and they're usually  tasks that are cognitively demanding  and usually these sessions would last  anywhere between one to four hours  and by comparison shallow work is  something that's not very cognitively  demanding  it's something that's usually very  logistical  in nature so things like scheduling  meetings  responding to emails responding to  messages  and they can usually be done in a state  of distraction  and he says they don't bring much new  value to the world and they're very  easily replicatable  so in the past couple of decades our  communication tools  like social media instant messaging  meetings open offices have  exploded but our  ability to be selective on these tools  have  not he says that we  as long as these tools offer some  benefit  we don't really carefully consider  the negatives of these tools and most  companies  encourage multitasking they  expect a speedy response to emails  most companies have a lot of meetings  and especially as you get into more  senior roles you're  managing a number of different projects  rather than focusing on just one  and you end up doing a lot more  logistical tasks we don't actually have  the metrics  to tell us how this is hurting our  bottom line  and he calls this the principle of least  resistance  which means that without clear feedback  on how  something is hurting our business we  tend to just go with the behaviors that  are  the easiest in the moment and he says  that without a very clear idea of what  productivity means  our workforce goes back to this very old  industrial idea of productivity which is  doing a lot of stuff in a very  visible manner so for example if you  respond to  a lot of emails very quickly it's very  visible to everyone that you're on top  of it  as opposed to if you're researching on a  specific topic it might be hard for you  to show the results of that in the next  couple hours  and also shallow work is easier it  basically creates this illusion  of productivity um but doesn't actually  add that much value  and is very easily replicatable so what  this means is that  we live in a world where our attention  is very fractured  and the average person does very little  deep work on  a daily basis so apparently if you  constantly spend your time  in this state of shallow work your brain  permanently loses the capacity to do the  work  so the science behind that is that  there's apparently these things called  myelin in our brains and so whenever  you're doing a specific task  myelin builds up around the neurons that  are connected  to performing that specific task  and so this allows the circuits to  fire more smoothly um and so when you  keep  practicing one skill um  these circuits fire over and over again  and so  um you create this like layer of myelin  around the neurons and so this is how  the brain  basically cements a skill and so  apparently it takes years and years of  practice  um to get into the habit of  doing deep work and to increase  performance  and so that's really the difference  between  like your average joe and your  like expert performer  he also talks about this idea of  attention residue  so when you switch between tasks your  attention doesn't immediately switch  there's a part of your brain that's  still on the  task that you were doing before and  that's probably why so many productivity  gurus  encourage um time batching so  doing like a similar set of tasks all at  the same time  and like limiting context switching  so what this all means is i um to  perform at your peak level  you need like full concentration  without any distractions um and  to be able to work for an extended  period of time so how do you actually do  deep work  so he provides five steps that you can  implement into your life  um and basically there they design your  environment  and your life so that it makes doing  deep work a lot easier  so i've tried a couple of these myself  and  they've made a very positive change in  my life and i would  highly suggest you try them too but i  would also suggest maybe trying  one at a time because you know you don't  want to try  doing all of them and get discouraged  and  stop doing them right so number one  learn how to categorize tools  as deep or shallow so first you try to  imagine a goal or a desired outcome  in whatever aspects of your life it  could be your personal life it can be in  your professional life  for each of the tools that are consuming  your time  and energy ask yourself like is this  really helping me achieve my desired  outcome  and if the answer is no  he suggests quit the tool  which is obviously not super easy to do  but that is his suggestion very extreme  i know number two make quick structural  changes that encourages  deep work so this is a so this is  actually a pretty common trick that you  do  if you're trying to build new habits you  basically  design your environment so that it's  really hard for you to  get distracted and so here are some of  the structural changes that  cal newport recommends number one  disable all calls messages and  notifications  because nothing is that urgent  number two become hard to reach set auto  response to email  so that people don't have an expectation  that you're gonna respond  immediately number three quit social  media  he says just try quitting for 30 days  and don't even tell people that you're  gonna be leaving social media because  actually nobody will care  so you might have noticed that i haven't  posted anything on instagram for over a  month  and that is partially because i didn't  really have anything to post  but also because i was trying this  little experiment  and it has freed up a lot of free time  for me because  i used to whenever i was on transit i  used to go on instagram and scroll  through instagram  but now i listen to audiobooks so  i finished reading this book  this month i finished reading grit  by angela duckworth i also finished  reading the four hour work week  um so it's been a good good uh  transformation for me number four work  in a quiet place  i don't know who thought this open plan  office was a good idea  but most of our offices are now open  floor plans  and so it can be kind of hard to get  some focused work done  he suggests going to a private room or  the library to get your work done  or using noise cancelling headphones  number five  work at a quiet time so you might  remember from my  other video where i was talking about  trying to build up the habit of waking  up at six a.m  so i actually recently pushed my time to  5 a.m it was hard so i do my writing  uh just right in the morning and i  actually get to work around  7 a.m so i have a full hour and a half  where i'm in the office by myself  and so that's probably where i get the  most of my work done  in the day just being able to  not have any distractions for an hour  and a half  has been a total game changer number six  limit internet access during deep work  time  um so he suggests to gather everything  you need  before you start your uh deep work  session  um but i mean yeah i think this one's  probably  the most tricky because uh for  most research you need to use the  internet  he also suggests blocking off  websites that you know are problematic  for you  for me that would be facebook number  seven get visibility on  deep versus shallow time by minuting the  day  so he says that the best way to change  something  is to measure it so  he suggests keeping a log of everything  that you're doing  at like five to ten minute intervals  and so at the end of the day you can go  back and assess  how much time you spend being distracted  and how much time you  spent actually being productive and  focused number eight decide on a fixed  time when you finish your work and try  to stick to it  because you know shutting down for the  day means you're giving your brain some  time to relax  which will definitely help you focus for  the next day  this is probably one of the hardest  things to do because a lot of times your  work  just kind of bleeds into your day um  but yeah he says just really try to  commit to  a end of work time and  just really stick to it and the ninth  step  is train your attention so a lot of  these steps before  were ways you can eliminate shallow work  from your life  but he also suggests to actively  practice deep work and actively  practice focusing on a daily basis it's  kind of like meditation  you know as you're focusing on your task  if you see your attention  wander off just gently bring it back to  the initial object  he also suggests to become familiar  and comfortable with boredom so if  you're standing in the line  don't just immediately pull out your  phone to look at  things to do um he says just  you know just stand there and just  learn to be bored and the other thing he  suggests is practice thinking while  you're walking  which sounds like such a strange idea  but we usually  tend to listen to music or listen to  audio books while we're walking  and i feel like with the new  technologies  it be it has become rarer and rarer for  me  to just have moments where i'm walking  in silence  with my own thoughts and so i actually  started doing that more actively and  um i found it kind of hard  to just walk and think like i find that  it's  much easier for me to write down my  ideas where i can visually see them  but i can really see the value of this  practice so this is something that i'm  trying  to integrate a little bit more into my  life and then he also suggests  giving little workout exercises for your  mind  so for example learning a memorization  trick  or learning a new language  there is actually a really great forum  that i follow it's called the art of  memory  and people just like memory nerds talk  about their different  memory techniques and um i think  like 10 years ago i bought this course  called p  memory where you can basically train  yourself how to  have photographic memory  and i actually never ended up completing  the course because it was just like  way way way too tedious after a couple  of courses i could visibly  see the increase in my level of focus  because  it just required you to um  like a huge part of memory is just being  able to focus  and so that was it that was my summary  of the book deep work  and if you're interested in this topic i  would highly  recommend reading the full book because  he actually offers a lot of really  interesting  studies and examples and  stories of people who's um  really embodied this idea of deep work  in their lives and  how that has impacted their careers and  their professional lives  and their personal lives so yeah it's a  it's a good  uh fairly quick read i would love to get  your thoughts on this  um do you try to actively implement deep  work into your life  and um what do you think are the skills  that are gonna be super valuable and  rare in the next five to ten years  if you enjoy this video please give me a  thumbs up  it